1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,157
This is MATH 239, video 17 b. The topic is quadratic recurrences and the Catalan numbers, section 4.4 of the course notes.

2
00:00:11,607 --> 00:00:31,618
After this video, you can solve a quadratic equation for a generating series and choose the correct sign. You can use the general binomial series. You can prove Proposition 4.22, count well-formed parenthesizations, and solve Exercise 4.12 and a practice question.

3
00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:50,201
In video 15 we saw: g satisfies a homogeneous linear recurrence exactly when G of x is P over Q. We can write this as Q of x times G of x, minus P of x, equals 0. This is a linear equation for G of x.

4
00:00:50,651 --> 00:01:09,446
Definition 4.19. g satisfies a quadratic recurrence if G of x solves a quadratic equation. A of x times G of x squared, plus B of x times G of x, plus C of x, equals 0. Here A, B and C are power series.

5
00:01:09,896 --> 00:01:23,720
Why is this a recurrence? G of x squared has products of coefficients. The grid shows g i times g j. The cell in row i and column j belongs to x to the i plus j.

6
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:40,063
The coefficient of x cubed in G squared is the sum on the purple diagonal, where i plus j equals 3. So the coefficient of x to the n in the equation gives g sub n from sums of products of earlier terms.

7
00:01:40,513 --> 00:01:53,612
Solve the quadratic equation with the Quadratic Formula. G plus or minus equals minus B, plus or minus the square root of B squared minus 4 A C, all over 2 A.

8
00:01:54,062 --> 00:02:01,977
We need the square root of a power series. The general binomial series gives it. The next slides show this series.

9
00:02:02,427 --> 00:02:18,107
The formula gives two solutions. A generating series of combinatorial objects has only nonnegative coefficients and nonnegative exponents. The notes say: in general, only one of the two solutions is correct.

10
00:02:18,557 --> 00:02:29,010
So we keep the solution with these properties. The notes say that CO 330 gives the full justification of this algebra with power series.

11
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:51,903
Definition 4.20. For any complex number alpha and any natural number k, alpha choose k is 1 over k factorial, times alpha, times alpha minus 1, and so on, down to alpha minus k plus 1. For k equal to 0, the product is empty, so alpha choose 0 is 1.

12
00:02:52,353 --> 00:03:02,166
Take alpha equal to one half. For k equal to 1, there is one factor, one half. Divide by 1 factorial. The result is one half.

13
00:03:02,616 --> 00:03:13,368
For k equal to 2, the factors are one half and minus one half. The product is minus one quarter. Divide by 2. The result is minus one eighth.

14
00:03:13,818 --> 00:03:23,418
For k equal to 3, add the factor minus three halves. The product is three eighths. Divide by 6. The result is one sixteenth.

15
00:03:23,868 --> 00:03:44,220
For k equal to 4, add minus five halves. Divide by 24. The result is minus 5 over 128. There are k factors, so alpha choose k is a polynomial in alpha of degree k. For a natural number alpha it is the usual binomial coefficient.

16
00:03:44,670 --> 00:04:03,955
Theorem 4.21, the Binomial Series. For any complex number alpha, 1 plus x to the alpha equals the sum over k of alpha choose k, x to the k. The notes give a sketch of the proof with Taylor's Theorem. Start with the function 1 plus x to the alpha.

17
00:04:04,405 --> 00:04:09,589
Take the derivative. It is alpha times 1 plus x to the alpha minus 1.

18
00:04:10,039 --> 00:04:25,591
Each derivative brings down the exponent as a new factor. The k th derivative is alpha, times alpha minus 1, and so on, times alpha minus k plus 1, times 1 plus x to the alpha minus k.

19
00:04:26,041 --> 00:04:43,364
Put x equal to 0, so 1 plus x to any power is 1. Divide by k factorial. The Taylor coefficient is alpha choose k. The function is analytic for x of absolute value less than 1, so this Taylor series is correct.

20
00:04:43,814 --> 00:05:00,155
Use the theorem with alpha equal to one half. The coefficients are the numbers from the last slide. Check the result: square the series. The grid has the products of the coefficients. The diagonal for x to the 0 has one cell, 1.

21
00:05:00,605 --> 00:05:06,600
The diagonal for x to the 1 has two cells, one half and one half. The sum is 1.

22
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:14,623
The diagonal for x squared gives minus one eighth, plus one quarter, minus one eighth. The sum is 0.

23
00:05:15,073 --> 00:05:18,508
The diagonal for x cubed also sums to 0.

24
00:05:18,958 --> 00:05:26,126
The diagonal for x to the 4 also sums to 0. So the square is 1 plus x, as it must be.

25
00:05:26,576 --> 00:05:41,680
Proposition 4.22 gives the series of the square root of 1 minus 4 x. The proof starts with Theorem 4.21. Put alpha equal to one half, and put minus 4 x in place of x.

26
00:05:42,130 --> 00:05:46,567
The first row is one half choose k, for k from 0 to 5.

27
00:05:47,017 --> 00:05:49,641
The second row is minus 4 to the k.

28
00:05:50,091 --> 00:06:07,051
Multiply the columns. The coefficients are 1, minus 2, minus 2, minus 4, minus 10, minus 28. For k equal to 0 the coefficient is 1. The next slide shows the general formula for k at least 1.

29
00:06:07,501 --> 00:06:19,085
For k at least 1, write out one half choose k. There are k factors: one half, minus one half, minus three halves, and so on. The picture shows k equal to 4.

30
00:06:19,535 --> 00:06:32,485
The k minus 1 factors after the first are negative. Together with minus 1 to the k, one minus sign is left. So the result is negative, and every factor becomes positive.

31
00:06:32,935 --> 00:06:52,263
Each of the k factors has a 2 in the denominator. 4 to the k over 2 to the k is 2 to the k. Write one 2 out as 2 to the k minus 1 times 2, and use the 2 for the factor one half. The numerators are 1, 1, 3, 5, up to 2 k minus 3.

32
00:06:52,713 --> 00:07:10,953
Now multiply and divide by the even numbers 2, 4, up to 2 k minus 2. The odd numbers times the even numbers give 2 k minus 2 factorial. For k equal to 4, 1, 3, 5 times 2, 4, 6 is 6 factorial.

33
00:07:11,403 --> 00:07:39,093
Each even number is 2 times a number from 1 to k minus 1. So the product of the even numbers is 2 to the k minus 1, times k minus 1 factorial. Also k factorial is k times k minus 1 factorial. The result is minus 2 over k, times 2 k minus 2 choose k minus 1. For k equal to 4, this is minus 2 over 4 times 20, which is minus 10.

34
00:07:39,543 --> 00:07:56,823
The notes ask a question: where did we use the fact that k is at least 1? For k equal to 0, the coefficient is 1. The formula 2 over k, times 2 k minus 2 choose k minus 1, has no meaning at k equal to 0.

35
00:07:57,273 --> 00:08:18,265
The calculation takes out the first factor, one half, and keeps one minus sign. For k equal to 0 there are no factors. The last lines also use k minus 1 factorial and divide by k. So the formula covers only k at least 1, and the proposition writes the term 1 separately.

36
00:08:18,715 --> 00:08:29,446
The Catalan numbers are C n equals 1 over n plus 1, times 2 n choose n. The table shows C 0 to C 10, as in the notes.

37
00:08:29,896 --> 00:08:37,704
For example, C 3 is one quarter of 6 choose 3. 6 choose 3 is 20, so C 3 is 5.

38
00:08:38,154 --> 00:08:47,711
C 4 is one fifth of 8 choose 4. That is 70 over 5, which is 14. The next example counts objects with these numbers.

39
00:08:48,161 --> 00:09:10,903
Example 4.23. A well-formed parenthesization, or W F P, is a sequence of n opening and n closing parentheses that match together by the usual rules. Its size is the number of opening parentheses. Here are the five W F Ps of size 3. The arcs join the matching pairs.

40
00:09:11,353 --> 00:09:17,475
Each closing parenthesis matches the nearest opening one to its left that has no match yet.

41
00:09:17,925 --> 00:09:35,867
Here are the fourteen W F Ps of size 4, as in the notes. The counts 5 and 14 are Catalan numbers. Let w sub n be the number of W F Ps of size n. The goal is to find w sub n for every n.

42
00:09:36,317 --> 00:09:40,925
How do we test that a sequence matches? Here is a well-formed example.

43
00:09:41,375 --> 00:09:54,260
Read from left to right. Count plus 1 for each opening parenthesis and minus 1 for each closing one. Call the count the depth. Here it never goes below 0, and it ends at 0.

44
00:09:54,710 --> 00:10:04,758
In the second sequence, the third symbol makes the depth minus 1. This closing parenthesis has no partner. So the sequence is not well-formed.

45
00:10:05,208 --> 00:10:10,499
Take any W F P gamma that is not empty. Here gamma has size 5.

46
00:10:10,949 --> 00:10:14,682
It begins with an opening parenthesis. It is orange.

47
00:10:15,132 --> 00:10:22,087
Exactly one closing parenthesis matches it. It is at the first place where the depth returns to 0.

48
00:10:22,537 --> 00:10:46,601
So gamma is an opening parenthesis, then alpha, then the matching closing parenthesis, then beta. Alpha and beta are W F Ps themselves, and either one can be empty. Here alpha has size 2 and beta has size 2. The size of gamma is 1 plus the size of alpha plus the size of beta: 5 equals 1 plus 2 plus 2.

49
00:10:47,051 --> 00:11:03,328
Look at the five W F Ps of size 3, split as open, alpha, close, beta. Alpha is blue and beta is purple. When alpha has size 0, beta has size 2. There are 1 times 2 such W F Ps.

50
00:11:03,778 --> 00:11:08,706
When alpha has size 1, beta has size 1. There is 1 times 1.

51
00:11:09,156 --> 00:11:13,615
When alpha has size 2, beta is empty. There are 2 times 1.

52
00:11:14,065 --> 00:11:29,190
The converse also holds: for any W F Ps alpha and beta, open, alpha, close, beta is a W F P. So the split is a bijection. The count is 2 plus 1 plus 2, which is 5.

53
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:41,373
The notes write 0 for an opening parenthesis and 1 for a closing one. Then a W F P is a binary string. Let script W be the set of these strings.

54
00:11:41,823 --> 00:11:58,186
The decomposition gives the recursive expression: script W equals epsilon, or 0, W, 1, W. The last two slides show that the split of each string is unique. So the recursive expression is unambiguous.

55
00:11:58,636 --> 00:12:11,671
Let W of x be the generating series of W F Ps with respect to size. Split the sum. The empty string has size 0. It gives x to the 0, which is 1.

56
00:12:12,121 --> 00:12:26,201
Every other gamma is open, alpha, close, beta, for exactly one pair alpha, beta. Its size is 1 plus the size of alpha plus the size of beta. So the sum runs over all pairs.

57
00:12:26,651 --> 00:12:42,757
The double sum is x times the sum over alpha, times the sum over beta. Each of these sums is W of x. So W of x equals 1 plus x times W of x squared. This is a quadratic equation.

58
00:12:43,207 --> 00:12:49,927
Take the coefficient of x to the n on both sides, for n at least 1. The 1 gives nothing.

59
00:12:50,377 --> 00:13:08,788
x times W squared moves the coefficients one place. So w sub n is the sum over i from 0 to n minus 1 of w i times w n minus 1 minus i. This is the quadratic recurrence. w 0 is 1.

60
00:13:09,238 --> 00:13:23,851
The grid shows the products w i times w j. For w 4, add the diagonal where i plus j equals 3: 5 plus 2 plus 2 plus 5. So w 4 is 14.

61
00:13:24,301 --> 00:13:36,035
In the same way, w 1 is 1, w 2 is 2, and w 3 is 5. Each term of the sum counts the W F Ps whose alpha has size i.

62
00:13:36,485 --> 00:13:59,802
Write the equation as x W squared minus W plus 1 equals 0. Here A is x, B is minus 1, and C is 1. The Quadratic Formula gives two solutions: 1 plus or minus the square root of 1 minus 4 x, over 2 x. The top row is the series of the square root, from Proposition 4.22.

63
00:14:00,252 --> 00:14:13,820
With the plus sign, the numerator starts with 2. Division by 2 x gives 1 over x, a negative exponent. The other coefficients are negative. This cannot count objects. Reject it.

64
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:34,409
With the minus sign, the constant terms cancel. The numerator is 0, 2, 2, 4, 10, 28. Division by 2 x moves every coefficient one place to the left and halves it. The result is 1, 1, 2, 5, 14. These are the counts of W F Ps.

65
00:14:34,859 --> 00:14:52,309
Now the exact formula. By Proposition 4.22, 1 minus the square root of 1 minus 4 x is 2 times the sum over k at least 1 of 1 over k, times 2 k minus 2 choose k minus 1, x to the k.

66
00:14:52,759 --> 00:14:58,391
Divide by 2 x. The 2 cancels, and every power of x drops by 1.

67
00:14:58,841 --> 00:15:17,913
Put n equal to k minus 1. Then k is n plus 1, and 2 k minus 2 is 2 n. So w sub n is 1 over n plus 1, times 2 n choose n. The number of W F Ps of size n is the n th Catalan number.

68
00:15:18,363 --> 00:15:24,294
The generating series W of x has a square root. It is not a rational function.

69
00:15:24,744 --> 00:15:44,413
Theorem 3.13 says that an unambiguous regular expression gives a rational generating series. The notes conclude that the set of W F Ps is not a rational language. Regular expressions cannot describe it, but the recursive expression can.

70
00:15:44,863 --> 00:15:57,151
Exercise 4.12 part a. Pause the video and try it. Show that 1 over the square root of 1 minus 4 x is the sum of 2 k choose k, x to the k.

71
00:15:57,601 --> 00:16:16,140
Use Theorem 4.21 with alpha equal to minus one half, and minus 4 x in place of x. The coefficient of x to the k is minus one half choose k, times minus 4 to the k. The rows show both numbers for k from 0 to 4.

72
00:16:16,590 --> 00:16:33,550
Write out the k factors. Each factor is negative, so the k minus signs cancel the minus 1 to the k. 4 to the k over 2 to the k is 2 to the k. The numerators are the odd numbers 1, 3, up to 2 k minus 1.

73
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:44,923
2 to the k times k factorial is the product of the even numbers 2, 4, up to 2 k. Multiply the top and the bottom by k factorial.

74
00:16:45,373 --> 00:17:04,509
The odd numbers times the even numbers give 2 k factorial. So the coefficient is 2 k factorial over k factorial squared, which is 2 k choose k. The last row gives 1, 2, 6, 20, 70. For k equal to 0, both sides are 1.

75
00:17:04,959 --> 00:17:17,033
Part b. Pause the video and try it. Show that the sum over j of 2 j choose j, times 2 n minus 2 j choose n minus j, equals 4 to the n.

76
00:17:17,483 --> 00:17:29,238
Square the series from part a. The square of 1 over the square root of 1 minus 4 x is 1 over 1 minus 4 x. Its coefficients are 4 to the n.

77
00:17:29,688 --> 00:17:47,160
By the product formula, the coefficient of x to the n in the square is the sum over j of 2 j choose j, times 2 n minus 2 j choose n minus j. The grid shows the products. The purple diagonal is n equal to 3.

78
00:17:47,610 --> 00:18:00,239
Two equal series have equal coefficients. So the sum equals 4 to the n. For n equal to 3: 20 plus 12 plus 12 plus 20 is 64, which is 4 cubed.

79
00:18:00,689 --> 00:18:07,708
Part c asks for a combinatorial proof. The notes give no answer, and this video does not cover it.

80
00:18:08,158 --> 00:18:22,174
A practice question. Pause the video and try it. A power series H of x satisfies H equals 1 plus x H plus x squared H squared. Part a: find a recurrence and the first terms.

81
00:18:22,624 --> 00:18:40,907
Take the coefficient of x to the n. The 1 gives h 0 equals 1. x H gives h n minus 1. x squared H squared gives the sum over i of h i times h n minus 2 minus i. The sum is empty for n equal to 1.

82
00:18:41,357 --> 00:18:51,853
For example, h 4 is h 3 plus the diagonal where i plus j equals 2. That is 4 plus 2 plus 1 plus 2, which is 9.

83
00:18:52,303 --> 00:18:57,252
So the first terms are 1, 1, 2, 4, 9, 21, 51.

84
00:18:57,702 --> 00:19:20,145
Part b. Move all terms to one side. x squared H squared, plus x minus 1 times H, plus 1, equals 0. So A is x squared, B is x minus 1, and C is 1. B squared minus 4 A C is 1 minus 2 x minus 3 x squared. The row shows its square root, from the binomial series.

85
00:19:20,595 --> 00:19:32,349
The Quadratic Formula gives two solutions. With the plus sign, the numerator starts with 2. Division by 2 x squared gives a term x to the minus 2. Reject it.

86
00:19:32,799 --> 00:19:52,426
So H is 1 minus x minus the square root, over 2 x squared. Check: for n at least 0, h sub n is minus one half times the coefficient of x to the n plus 2 in the square root. This gives 1, 1, 2, 4, 9, the same values as part a.

87
00:19:52,876 --> 00:20:06,615
To recap. A quadratic recurrence means a quadratic equation for the generating series. Solve it with the Quadratic Formula, and keep the solution with nonnegative exponents and coefficients.

88
00:20:07,065 --> 00:20:24,046
The general binomial coefficient and Theorem 4.21 give the square roots. Proposition 4.22 gives the series of the square root of 1 minus 4 x, and Exercise 4.12 gives the series of 1 over it.

89
00:20:24,496 --> 00:20:41,584
Well-formed parenthesizations split as open, alpha, close, beta. This gives W equals 1 plus x W squared, and the number of size n is the Catalan number C n. Video 18 is the practice midterm walkthrough.
